WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.140
Did the drought reshape these finches, or change which birds remained?

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.020
On Daphne Major, most medium ground finches died during a severe drought.

3
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.620
Researchers had measured their beaks beforehand.

4
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.480
The survivors had deeper beaks on average.

5
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.340
Watch the population, not one changing bird.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:26.583
Before the drought, beak depth varied.

7
00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:30.163
Beak depth is the height of the beak at its base.

8
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:36.323
Blue birds had shallower beaks, yellow birds had middle beaks, and green birds had

9
00:00:36.323 --> 00:00:41.543
deeper beaks. These differences existed before conditions changed.

10
00:00:42.903 --> 00:00:45.503
Variation gives the environment something to sort.

11
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:48.287
Then drought changed the food.

12
00:00:49.847 --> 00:00:53.487
Small, soft seeds became scarce, while large, hard seeds remained.

13
00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:56.827
Deeper beaks could crack those hard seeds.

14
00:00:58.147 --> 00:01:01.807
Which birds do you predict will contribute more young to the next generation?

15
00:01:03.327 --> 00:01:05.047
Follow the colours already on screen.

16
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:15.953
More green birds survive in this simple picture, but some birds of every colour

17
00:01:15.953 --> 00:01:18.713
remain. The groups overlap.

18
00:01:19.973 --> 00:01:24.773
A deep beak improves the chance of survival here; it does not guarantee survival.

19
00:01:26.093 --> 00:01:28.933
A helpful trait depends on the present environment.

20
00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:33.037
Survival alone does not finish the mechanism.

21
00:01:34.497 --> 00:01:39.277
Beak size is strongly inherited, so parents and young tend to resemble each other.

22
00:01:40.537 --> 00:01:41.557
The survivors breed.

23
00:01:42.937 --> 00:01:47.737
Their young form another mixed population, now with deeper beaks more common on

24
00:01:47.737 --> 00:01:53.457
average. That makes change possible across generations, not within one life.

25
00:01:54.517 --> 00:01:57.197
Researchers did not guess which birds survived.

26
00:01:58.537 --> 00:02:01.557
Each measured bird had a numbered band before the drought.

27
00:02:02.857 --> 00:02:05.517
Later, the team checked which measured birds remained.

28
00:02:06.957 --> 00:02:10.117
Comparing the groups connects earlier beak depth with survival.

29
00:02:11.357 --> 00:02:15.037
It shows an association, not a promise about every bird.

30
00:02:16.357 --> 00:02:19.457
Parents and young provide separate inheritance evidence.

31
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.380
This is natural selection.

32
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.820
It needs variation, inheritance, and unequal reproductive success.

33
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.440
Inherited traits spread when their carriers leave more reproducing offspring.

34
00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:37.980
Fitness means that contribution to the next generation.

35
00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.300
It does not simply mean strength, health, or a long life.

36
00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:49.293
Mutation supplies new gene versions, and sexual reproduction shuffles them into new

37
00:02:49.293 --> 00:02:55.393
combinations. Selection does not produce a trait because organisms need it.

38
00:02:56.253 --> 00:03:01.433
It sorts inherited variation that is present, including mutations as they arise.

39
00:03:02.393 --> 00:03:04.513
No finch changed its own beak.

40
00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:07.437
The advantage is not permanent.

41
00:03:08.697 --> 00:03:12.477
If rainfall and seeds change, selection can change direction.

42
00:03:14.057 --> 00:03:17.417
Chance and arrivals from other populations can shift traits too.

43
00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:20.457
The drought did not reshape any beak.

44
00:03:21.537 --> 00:03:23.777
It changed which birds survived and bred.

45
00:03:24.917 --> 00:03:27.797
The next generation carried the population change.
